{"hash":"8637323b8fe8852c","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":81.681,"said":"On this upper storey you meet Shri Mahakaleshwar. If you have been to Ujjain, you may smile: there, Mahakal sits at the bottom, below the earth, with Omkareshwar above him. Here it is the reverse, Mahakal above and Omkareshwar below, and darshan on this floor is counted part of a complete pilgrimage. Higher still are the shrines of Siddhnath and Gupteshwar, and at the very top, the lord of the temple’s flag. Now think of the island itself, because it carries a king’s name. Temple tradition tells of Mandhata, a king of the Ikshvaku line, the same solar dynasty into which Lord Rama would later be born. He came to this hill and sat in meditation, and Shiva appeared and blessed him. And so the Lord dwells here, and the island has been called Mandhata ever since. The Skanda Purana, in its chapters praising the holy places along the Narmada, knows this island by an even older name: Vaidurya Mani Parvat, the mountain of the vaidurya gem. Now it is time to go down, to the heart of it all. The sanctum is below.\n\nDid you know? The Narmada parikrama, a walk of about 2,600 km along both banks of the river, passes only one Jyotirlinga: Omkareshwar.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1017},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1033,"end":1153}],"words":[[0.098,0.18,0,2],[0.29,0.204,3,7],[0.507,0.252,8,13],[0.771,0.469,14,20],[1.252,0.132,21,24],[1.396,0.252,25,29],[1.661,0.228,30,34],[1.901,0.925,35,48],[3.738,0.204,50,52],[3.954,0.18,53,56],[4.147,0.216,57,61],[4.375,0.216,62,66],[4.603,0.156,67,69],[4.772,0.577,70,76],[5.553,0.156,78,81],[5.721,0.192,82,85],[5.926,0.505,86,91],[6.635,0.3,93,98],[7.139,0.553,100,107],[7.704,0.312,108,112],[8.029,0.096,113,115],[8.137,0.084,116,119],[8.233,0.493,120,126],[8.966,0.3,128,133],[9.279,0.168,134,137],[9.459,0.288,138,143],[9.964,0.192,145,149],[10.168,0.781,150,161],[10.962,0.312,162,167],[11.286,0.337,168,171],[12.536,0.409,173,177],[1